WEBVTT

00:00:05.382 --> 00:00:08.364
Oxford Book Worms, Stage 1.

00:00:08.424 --> 00:00:13.088
The Coldest Place on Earth by Tim Vickery.

00:00:13.148 --> 00:00:15.669
Read by Mark Straker.

00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:22.224
Published in Copyright, Oxford University Press, 1997.

00:00:22.284 --> 00:00:32.352
Chapter 1 Two Ships The race began in the summer of 1910.

00:00:32.411 --> 00:00:44.022
On June 1st in London, a black ship, the Terranova, went down the river Thames to the sea.

00:00:44.082 --> 00:00:49.557
Thousands of people stood by the river to watch it.

00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:54.832
They were all excited and happy.

00:00:54.892 --> 00:01:03.869
On the Terranova, Captain Robert Falcon Scott, smiled quietly.

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:08.523
It was a very important day for him.

00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:17.568
He was a strong man, not very tall, in the blue clothes of a captain.

00:01:17.628 --> 00:01:26.464
He was 41 years old, but he had a young face like a boy.

00:01:26.524 --> 00:01:31.686
His eyes were dark and quiet.

00:01:31.746 --> 00:01:39.768
One man on the ship, Titus Oates, smiled at Scott.

00:01:39.828 --> 00:01:44.870
What an exciting day, Captain, he said.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.741
Look at those people.

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:52.012
I feel like an important man.

00:01:52.072 --> 00:01:54.664
Scott laughed.

00:01:54.724 --> 00:01:59.256
You are important, Titus, he said.

00:01:59.316 --> 00:02:03.202
And you're going to be famous too.

00:02:03.262 --> 00:02:05.778
We all are.

00:02:05.837 --> 00:02:09.029
Do you see this flag?

00:02:09.088 --> 00:02:17.504
He looked at the big British flag at the back of the ship and smiled at oats.

00:02:17.564 --> 00:02:22.836
That flag is coming with us, he said.

00:02:22.896 --> 00:02:28.820
In the Antarctic, I'm going to carry it under my clothes.

00:02:28.879 --> 00:02:33.910
We're going to be the first men at the South Pole.

00:02:33.969 --> 00:02:39.678
and that flag is going to be first, too.

00:02:39.738 --> 00:02:50.742
Five days later, on June 6th, a man opened the door of his wooden house in Norway.

00:02:50.802 --> 00:02:55.844
He was a tall man with a long face.

00:02:55.904 --> 00:03:00.306
He waited outside the house for a minute.

00:03:00.366 --> 00:03:04.410
Everything was very quiet.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:12.356
He could see no houses, only mountains, trees and water.

00:03:12.416 --> 00:03:15.146
It was nearly dark.

00:03:15.206 --> 00:03:19.878
The sky was black over the mountains.

00:03:19.938 --> 00:03:28.591
The man smiled and walked quickly away from the house, down to the sea.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:34.787
In the water, a big wooden ship waited for him.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:42.384
The man got onto the ship and talked and laughed quietly with his friends.

00:03:42.444 --> 00:03:50.878
The ship's name was Fram, and the man was Ruhold Amundsen.

00:03:50.938 --> 00:03:57.980
The Fram was the most beautiful ship on Earth, Amundsen thought.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:04.024
His friends were the best skiers on Earth, too.

00:04:04.084 --> 00:04:10.366
One of them, Olaf Bioland, smiled at him.

00:04:10.426 --> 00:04:16.478
North Pole, here we come, Captain," he said.

00:04:16.538 --> 00:04:19.919
Yes, Amundsen said.

00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:24.760
His friends could not see his face in the dark.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:29.806
Fram is going to the Arctic.

00:04:29.866 --> 00:04:37.794
Everyone on the Fram was ready to go to the North Pole to the Arctic.

00:04:37.854 --> 00:04:41.818
Amundsen wanted to go there too.

00:04:41.878 --> 00:04:46.701
But first, he wanted to go south.

00:04:46.761 --> 00:04:50.584
His friends didn't know that.

00:04:50.644 --> 00:04:59.744
At midnight on June 6th, the Fram moved quietly away from Amundsen's house.

00:04:59.804 --> 00:05:06.093
out to sea.

00:05:06.152 --> 00:05:20.604
Chapter 2 The Race The Fram went to an island in the south of Norway.

00:05:20.664 --> 00:05:32.344
It was a very little island with only one small wooden house, two trees and nearly a hundred dogs.

00:05:32.404 --> 00:05:36.806
Look at that, the Orland said.

00:05:36.866 --> 00:05:39.730
It's an island of dogs.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:48.236
There are dogs in the water, near the trees, on the house, dogs everywhere.

00:05:48.296 --> 00:05:51.838
Two men came out of the house.

00:05:51.898 --> 00:05:56.812
Harsell, Lindstrom, Amundsen said.

00:05:56.872 --> 00:05:59.350
It's good to see you.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:02.496
How many dogs do you have for me?"

00:06:02.556 --> 00:06:06.140
Ninety-nine roiled, said Harsle.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:10.012
The best ninety-nine dogs from Greenland.

00:06:10.072 --> 00:06:12.404
And they're very happy.

00:06:12.464 --> 00:06:14.015
They don't work.

00:06:14.075 --> 00:06:17.558
They just eat and play all day.

00:06:17.618 --> 00:06:20.941
They're having a wonderful summer here.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.381
Good.

00:06:22.441 --> 00:06:23.212
Good.

00:06:23.272 --> 00:06:24.782
Amundsen laughed.

00:06:24.842 --> 00:06:27.194
But that's finished now.

00:06:27.254 --> 00:06:29.460
Hey, you're on.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.074
Stop laughing.

00:06:31.134 --> 00:06:33.736
Come down here and help me.

00:06:33.796 --> 00:06:37.770
Let's get all these dogs onto the ship.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.482
It was not easy.

00:06:40.542 --> 00:06:47.138
The dogs were fat and strong, and they didn't want to go on the ship.

00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:55.535
But at last, after three hours hard work, all 99 were on the ship.

00:06:55.594 --> 00:06:59.996
And the Fram went out to see again.

00:07:00.056 --> 00:07:03.466
The men were not happy.

00:07:03.526 --> 00:07:05.568
The weather was bad.

00:07:05.628 --> 00:07:07.940
The dogs were dirty.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.122
And some of the men were ill.

00:07:11.182 --> 00:07:14.605
They began to ask questions.

00:07:14.665 --> 00:07:18.078
Why are we bringing dogs with us?

00:07:18.138 --> 00:07:21.000
Asked one man, Johansson.

00:07:21.059 --> 00:07:28.886
We're going thousands of kilometers south past Cape Horn and then north to Alaska.

00:07:28.946 --> 00:07:30.838
Why not wait?

00:07:30.898 --> 00:07:33.942
and to get dogs in Alaska.

00:07:34.001 --> 00:07:39.174
Don't ask me, said his friend, Helma Honson.

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:42.085
I don't understand it.

00:07:42.144 --> 00:07:45.977
The men talked for a long time.

00:07:46.036 --> 00:07:53.160
Then, on September 9th, Amundsen called everyone to the back of the ship.

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:57.162
He stood quietly and looked at them.

00:07:57.222 --> 00:08:00.922
Behind him was a big map.

00:08:00.982 --> 00:08:04.703
It was not a map of the Arctic.

00:08:04.762 --> 00:08:08.726
It was a map of Antarctica.

00:08:08.785 --> 00:08:14.210
The Orland looked at Helmer Hansen and laughed.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:18.343
Then Amundsen began to speak.

00:08:18.403 --> 00:08:24.548
Boys, he said, I know you are unhappy.

00:08:24.608 --> 00:08:31.060
You often ask me difficult questions, and I don't answer.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:38.862
Well, I'm going to answer all those questions now, today.

00:08:38.922 --> 00:08:44.274
We began to work for this journey two years ago.

00:08:44.334 --> 00:08:49.788
Then we wanted to be the first men at the North Pole.

00:08:49.808 --> 00:08:57.464
That last year Perry, an American, found the North Pole.

00:08:57.524 --> 00:09:04.917
So, America was first to the North Pole, not Norway.

00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:09.938
We're going there, but we're too late.

00:09:09.998 --> 00:09:15.200
I don't understand this, the Orland thought.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:23.942
Why is Amundsen talking about the North Pole, with a map of Antarctica behind him?

00:09:24.002 --> 00:09:27.442
Amundsen stopped for a minute.

00:09:27.502 --> 00:09:31.184
and looked at all the men slowly.

00:09:31.244 --> 00:09:36.548
No one said anything.

00:09:36.568 --> 00:09:43.112
We have to go a long way south before we get to Alaska, he said.

00:09:43.172 --> 00:09:47.556
Very near Antarctica, you know.

00:09:47.616 --> 00:09:55.289
And Captain Scott, the Englishman, is going to the South Pole this year.

00:09:55.349 --> 00:09:59.209
He wants to put his British flag there.

00:09:59.269 --> 00:10:06.504
An American flag at the North Pole, a British flag at the South Pole.

00:10:06.564 --> 00:10:10.866
The Orland began to understand.

00:10:10.926 --> 00:10:15.098
He started to smile and couldn't stop.

00:10:15.158 --> 00:10:19.020
He was warm and excited.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:24.360
Well boys, Amundsen said slowly.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:29.847
Do we want the British to put their flag at the South Pole first?

00:10:29.907 --> 00:10:33.228
How fast can we travel?

00:10:33.288 --> 00:10:40.159
We have a lot of dogs and some of the most wonderful skiers on Earth.

00:10:40.219 --> 00:10:44.500
The Orland here is the best in Norway.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:49.100
So, I have an idea, boys.

00:10:49.161 --> 00:10:52.537
Let's go to the South Pole.

00:10:52.597 --> 00:10:58.146
and put the Norwegian flag there before the British.

00:10:58.206 --> 00:11:01.347
What do you say?

00:11:01.407 --> 00:11:06.830
For a minute or two, it was very quiet.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:12.922
Amundsen waited, and the men watched him and thought.

00:11:12.982 --> 00:11:16.284
Then Beorland laughed.

00:11:16.344 --> 00:11:18.825
Yes, he said.

00:11:18.885 --> 00:11:20.366
Why not?

00:11:20.426 --> 00:11:22.980
It's a ski race, isn't it?

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.734
and the English can't ski.

00:11:25.794 --> 00:11:28.376
It's a wonderful idea.

00:11:28.436 --> 00:11:29.927
Of course!

00:11:29.987 --> 00:11:36.201
Let's go!

00:11:36.261 --> 00:11:54.064
Chapter 3 The Ponies On October 27th The Terranova arrived in Wellington, New Zealand.

00:11:54.124 --> 00:12:00.935
When Scott came off the ship, a newspaperman walked up to him.

00:12:00.996 --> 00:12:06.547
Captain Scott, Captain Scott, can I talk to you, please?

00:12:06.607 --> 00:12:08.428
He said.

00:12:08.488 --> 00:12:11.469
Scott stopped and smiled.

00:12:11.528 --> 00:12:15.140
Yes, of course, he said.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.852
What do you want to know?

00:12:17.912 --> 00:12:20.306
Are you going to win?

00:12:20.366 --> 00:12:22.146
The man asked.

00:12:22.206 --> 00:12:25.417
Win, Scott asked.

00:12:25.477 --> 00:12:27.748
Win what?

00:12:27.808 --> 00:12:32.159
Win the race to the south pole, of course.

00:12:32.219 --> 00:12:34.590
The newspaper man said.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:39.422
It's a race between you and Amundsen now.

00:12:39.482 --> 00:12:41.542
Look at this.

00:12:41.603 --> 00:12:45.084
He gave a newspaper to Scott.

00:12:45.144 --> 00:12:47.459
Scott looked at it.

00:12:47.519 --> 00:12:53.816
It said, Fram graces Scott to South Pole.

00:12:53.877 --> 00:12:58.549
We're going to win, says Amundsen.

00:12:58.609 --> 00:13:02.390
Scott's face went white.

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:06.262
Give me that, he said.

00:13:06.322 --> 00:13:11.025
He took the newspaper and read it carefully.

00:13:11.085 --> 00:13:15.994
The newspaperman watched him and waited.

00:13:16.054 --> 00:13:23.586
Well, Captain Scott, he said at blast, who's going to win this race?

00:13:23.646 --> 00:13:26.027
Tell me that.

00:13:26.087 --> 00:13:29.430
Scott looked at him angrily.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:33.103
This is stupid, he said.

00:13:33.163 --> 00:13:35.485
It's not a race.

00:13:35.545 --> 00:13:40.228
I came here to learn about the Antarctic.

00:13:40.288 --> 00:13:45.988
I'm not interested in Amundsen or in races.

00:13:46.048 --> 00:13:53.644
Then he walked back onto his ship with the newspaper in his hand.

00:13:53.704 --> 00:13:59.456
Later that day he talked to his men.

00:13:59.517 --> 00:14:04.398
He gave them the newspaper and laughed.

00:14:04.458 --> 00:14:08.190
It doesn't matter, he said.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:15.372
We're in front of Amundsen and we have more men and more money.

00:14:15.432 --> 00:14:20.665
He has only eight men and a lot of dogs.

00:14:20.725 --> 00:14:23.747
I know about dogs.

00:14:23.807 --> 00:14:27.190
They don't work in the Antarctic.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:32.543
We have sixteen men and the new motor sledges.

00:14:32.603 --> 00:14:34.914
They are much better.

00:14:34.974 --> 00:14:38.276
And tomorrow the ponies are coming.

00:14:38.336 --> 00:14:44.852
We need ponies, motor sledges, and good strong British men.

00:14:44.911 --> 00:14:46.565
That's all.

00:14:46.625 --> 00:14:49.329
Forget about Amundsen.

00:14:49.389 --> 00:14:52.630
He's not important.

00:14:52.690 --> 00:15:01.724
Scott asked oats to look after the ponies, but he did not let oats buy them.

00:15:01.784 --> 00:15:09.548
When oats first saw the ponies in New Zealand, he was very unhappy.

00:15:09.608 --> 00:15:16.327
Most of the ponies were old, and some of them were ill.

00:15:16.387 --> 00:15:21.385
Their beautiful ponies Titus, Scott said.

00:15:21.445 --> 00:15:23.915
They come from China.

00:15:23.975 --> 00:15:27.086
Their wonderful ponies.

00:15:27.146 --> 00:15:33.407
Oats looked at them angrily and said nothing.

00:15:33.467 --> 00:15:39.818
Then he asked, where is their food, Captain?

00:15:39.878 --> 00:15:43.738
Here, Scott opened a door.

00:15:43.798 --> 00:15:46.660
Oats looked inside.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.543
He thought for a minute.

00:15:49.603 --> 00:15:54.455
We need more food than this, Captain Scott.

00:15:54.515 --> 00:16:00.838
These ponies are going to work in the coldest place on Earth.

00:16:00.898 --> 00:16:06.780
They need a lot of food, more than this.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.702
Scott smiled quietly.

00:16:10.762 --> 00:16:15.154
We can't take more food on this ship, Titus.

00:16:15.214 --> 00:16:17.680
Where can we put it?

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:20.986
But it doesn't matter, old boy.

00:16:21.046 --> 00:16:24.709
They're very strong ponies, you know.

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:29.032
The best ponies on Earth.

00:16:29.092 --> 00:16:35.647
Later that night, Oates wrote a letter to his mother.

00:16:35.707 --> 00:16:42.041
There are 19 ponies on the Terranova now, he wrote.

00:16:42.102 --> 00:16:48.272
All the ponies are in a small room at the front of the ship.

00:16:48.332 --> 00:16:53.632
We eat our food in the room under the ponies.

00:16:53.692 --> 00:16:58.566
So our table is often wet and dirty.

00:16:58.626 --> 00:17:03.930
Scott makes a lot of mistakes, I think.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:13.791
And Antarctica is a very dangerous place.

00:17:13.851 --> 00:17:37.202
Chapter 4 Food Depos The two ships, Terranova and Fram, arrived in Antarctica in January 1911, at the end of summer.

00:17:37.262 --> 00:17:44.932
The Englishmen and the Norwegians wanted to stay on the ice all winter.

00:17:44.992 --> 00:17:53.802
They wanted to be ready to go to the south pole at the beginning of the next Antarctic summer.

00:17:53.862 --> 00:17:58.656
The dogs pulled the Norwegians sledges.

00:17:58.716 --> 00:18:06.972
They rang quickly over the snow and pulled the big sledges from the ship onto the ice.

00:18:07.032 --> 00:18:11.618
The men ran beside them on skis.

00:18:11.678 --> 00:18:17.124
They put a big wooden house on the ice.

00:18:17.184 --> 00:18:23.486
The house was full of food and skis and sledges.

00:18:23.546 --> 00:18:27.846
They called it Framheim.

00:18:27.906 --> 00:18:35.368
Outside the house, the dogs lived in holes under the snow.

00:18:35.428 --> 00:18:43.342
When the house was ready, the men made their first journey south.

00:18:43.402 --> 00:18:54.262
Before the winter, they wanted to take a lot of food south and leave it in depots.

00:18:54.322 --> 00:19:05.240
For the long journey to the Pole, they needed a lot of food, and they couldn't carry it all with them.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:20.622
On February 10th, five men, three sledges, eighteen dogs, and half a ton of food, left Framheim and went south.

00:19:20.683 --> 00:19:23.183
It was easy.

00:19:23.243 --> 00:19:32.526
The weather was warm for the Antarctic, between minus seven degrees centigrade and minus seventeen degrees centigrade.

00:19:32.586 --> 00:19:39.598
The snow was good, and the dogs and skis went fast.

00:19:39.658 --> 00:19:45.122
They went 50 or 60 kilometers every day.

00:19:45.182 --> 00:19:55.427
After four days, they reached 80 degrees south and made the first depot.

00:19:55.487 --> 00:20:00.690
Amundsen made his depot very carefully.

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:06.412
It was very important to find it again next summer.

00:20:06.471 --> 00:20:11.482
So he put a big black flag on top.

00:20:11.542 --> 00:20:25.487
Then he put ten flags to the east of the depot, each flag half a kilometer from the next and ten flags to the west.

00:20:25.547 --> 00:20:36.301
So there were flags for five kilometers to the left of the depot and five kilometers to the right.

00:20:36.361 --> 00:20:44.136
Then they went back to Framheim and took some more food south.

00:20:44.196 --> 00:20:49.628
This time to 82 degrees south.

00:20:49.688 --> 00:20:53.460
This time it was harder.

00:20:53.520 --> 00:21:00.124
The temperature was sometimes minus 40 degrees centigrade.

00:21:00.184 --> 00:21:05.152
And there were strong winds with a lot of snow.

00:21:05.212 --> 00:21:13.333
The dogs and men were very tired, and the tents and boots were bad.

00:21:13.393 --> 00:21:21.779
At the second depot, they put out sixty flags to help them find it again.

00:21:21.839 --> 00:21:27.413
They came back to Framheim on March 23rd.

00:21:27.473 --> 00:21:31.036
It was nearly winter in the Antarctic.

00:21:31.096 --> 00:21:34.620
Their ship, Fram, was far away now.

00:21:34.681 --> 00:21:37.304
near South America.

00:21:37.364 --> 00:21:41.634
They were alone on the ice.

00:21:41.694 --> 00:21:48.196
Oats went with Scott to make the first British depot.

00:21:48.256 --> 00:21:53.598
They left Cape Evans on January 25th.

00:21:53.658 --> 00:22:00.869
There were 13 men, 8 ponies and 26 dogs.

00:22:00.929 --> 00:22:04.696
The dogs were faster than the ponies.

00:22:04.756 --> 00:22:12.606
They ran quickly over the top of the snow, but the ponies' feet went through it.

00:22:12.666 --> 00:22:24.410
Every morning, the ponies started first, and the dogs started two hours later, because they ran faster.

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:34.268
At night, the dogs made warm holes under the snow, but the ponies stood on top of the snow.

00:22:34.328 --> 00:22:39.272
It was minus twenty degrees centigrade.

00:22:39.332 --> 00:22:44.846
After fifteen days, Oates talked to Scott.

00:22:44.906 --> 00:22:52.250
There was a strong wind, and the two men's faces were white with snow.

00:22:52.310 --> 00:22:56.712
Three of the ponies are ill, Captain.

00:22:56.772 --> 00:23:01.686
Oates said, they can't go on.

00:23:01.746 --> 00:23:04.906
Don't be stupid, Oates.

00:23:04.966 --> 00:23:12.576
Scott answered, they're good, strong animals, the best ponies on earth.

00:23:12.636 --> 00:23:17.227
Not these three, oats said.

00:23:17.287 --> 00:23:22.688
They're ill and unhappy, and now they can't walk.

00:23:22.748 --> 00:23:27.799
Let's kill them and leave the meat here in the snow.

00:23:27.859 --> 00:23:31.740
We can eat it, or the dogs can.

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:34.134
Of course not!

00:23:34.194 --> 00:23:36.248
Scott said angrily.

00:23:36.308 --> 00:23:39.270
These ponies are our friends.

00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:42.612
They work hard for us.

00:23:42.632 --> 00:23:46.144
I don't kill my friends.

00:23:46.204 --> 00:23:52.238
Three days later, two of the ponies were dead.

00:23:52.298 --> 00:23:56.912
Scott's men were slower than Amundsen's.

00:23:56.972 --> 00:24:03.086
It took them 24 days to get to 80 degrees south.

00:24:03.146 --> 00:24:11.508
They made a big depot there, and put one large black flag on top of it.

00:24:11.568 --> 00:24:15.949
Then they went back to Cape Evans.

00:24:16.009 --> 00:24:24.351
Their camp was on an island in the ice, and the sea ice moved sometimes.

00:24:24.411 --> 00:24:30.882
There were holes in the ice, and black sea water under it.

00:24:30.942 --> 00:24:39.826
One day Seven ponies went through the ice into the sea and died.

00:24:39.886 --> 00:24:49.160
One motor sledge also went into the sea.

00:24:49.220 --> 00:25:03.938
Chapter 5 A Long Cold Winter It was dark for four months.

00:25:03.998 --> 00:25:13.540
Outside the wooden house at Framheim, it was often minus sixty degrees centigrade.

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:18.902
The dogs lived in warm holes under the snow.

00:25:18.962 --> 00:25:26.423
The men stayed in the house and worked in their rooms under the snow.

00:25:26.484 --> 00:25:36.312
The skis and sledges came from the best shops in Norway, but Beorland wasn't happy with them.

00:25:36.372 --> 00:25:41.696
He changed a lot of things on the skis and sledges.

00:25:41.756 --> 00:25:46.570
Soon, the sledges were stronger than before.

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:51.603
The skis were better and faster too.

00:25:51.663 --> 00:25:55.696
All the Norwegians worked hard.

00:25:55.756 --> 00:26:04.942
They looked after their dogs, and worked on their equipment, the sledges, skis, tents.

00:26:05.002 --> 00:26:11.863
Every day, they thought about their journey to the pole and talked about it.

00:26:11.923 --> 00:26:17.494
And every day, Amundsen thought about Scott.

00:26:17.554 --> 00:26:23.836
One day, in midwinter, he talked to his men.

00:26:23.896 --> 00:26:27.906
Let's start early before Scott.

00:26:27.966 --> 00:26:29.696
Amazon said.

00:26:29.756 --> 00:26:37.090
Remember, Scott has more men than us, and he has motor sledges, too.

00:26:37.150 --> 00:26:40.832
Perhaps they can go faster than us.

00:26:40.893 --> 00:26:43.264
The Orland laughed.

00:26:43.324 --> 00:26:48.136
Oh, no, they can't go faster than me, he said.

00:26:48.196 --> 00:26:54.460
On snow, nothing can go faster than a good man on skis.

00:26:54.520 --> 00:26:58.501
We don't know, Amazon said.

00:26:58.562 --> 00:27:06.236
You're the best skier in Norway, but you get tired, and dogs get tired, too.

00:27:06.296 --> 00:27:09.988
Motor sledges don't get tired.

00:27:10.048 --> 00:27:13.892
They can go all day and all night.

00:27:13.952 --> 00:27:17.134
You, Hanson, laughed angrily.

00:27:17.194 --> 00:27:20.306
That's stupid, he said.

00:27:20.366 --> 00:27:26.942
Perhaps the motor sledges can go all night, but the Englishman can't.

00:27:27.002 --> 00:27:30.524
The English can't win, Roald.

00:27:30.585 --> 00:27:35.677
They don't understand snow, but we do.

00:27:35.737 --> 00:27:38.958
And they're too slow.

00:27:39.019 --> 00:27:45.402
Perhaps, Amundsen said, but I want to win this race.

00:27:45.462 --> 00:27:49.254
So we're going to start early.

00:27:49.314 --> 00:27:51.645
Do you understand?

00:27:51.705 --> 00:27:56.146
It was quiet and warm inside Framheim.

00:27:56.206 --> 00:28:04.411
Viorland looked at Amundsen and thought about the long, cold journey in front of him.

00:28:04.471 --> 00:28:14.209
He thought about the dogs in their holes under the snow and listened to the wind over the house.

00:28:14.269 --> 00:28:25.332
When Ruhald, he said quietly, on August 24th, the sun comes back on that day.

00:28:25.392 --> 00:28:28.288
We start then.

00:28:28.348 --> 00:28:32.690
But we can't, Johansson said.

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:36.052
He looked angry and unhappy.

00:28:36.112 --> 00:28:38.732
That's too early.

00:28:38.792 --> 00:28:41.034
We can't start then.

00:28:41.094 --> 00:28:45.336
It's dangerous and stupid.

00:28:45.396 --> 00:28:49.308
Amundsen looked at Johansson coldly.

00:28:49.368 --> 00:28:53.523
You're wrong, Johansson, he said.

00:28:53.583 --> 00:28:56.697
We want to win, remember?

00:28:56.757 --> 00:29:00.888
So we start on August 24th.

00:29:00.948 --> 00:29:07.430
Be all armed, listen to the winter wind outside.

00:29:07.490 --> 00:29:18.434
In Scott's camp at Cape Evans, no one talked about Amundsen, and no one worked hard.

00:29:18.494 --> 00:29:24.211
They had good food, and they played football on the snow.

00:29:24.271 --> 00:29:31.623
They wrote a newspaper, the South Polar Times, and read books.

00:29:31.683 --> 00:29:37.548
No one learned to ski, no one worked on the motor sledges.

00:29:37.608 --> 00:29:42.672
Twice, men went for long journeys across the snow.

00:29:42.732 --> 00:29:47.147
They walked and pulled the sledges themselves.

00:29:47.207 --> 00:29:52.868
Oats stayed at Cape Evans and looked after his ponies.

00:29:52.928 --> 00:30:00.131
Over the window in Cape Evans, Scott put a map of Antarctica.

00:30:00.191 --> 00:30:11.410
With a pen, he made a line from Cape Evans to the south pole, and he put a little British flag at the pole.

00:30:11.471 --> 00:30:18.156
Under the map, Scott wrote the day for the start of their journey.

00:30:18.216 --> 00:30:27.120
We start on November 3rd, he wrote.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:41.722
Chapter 6 A Bad Start On August 23rd, the Norwegian sledges were ready.

00:30:41.782 --> 00:30:47.494
They took them outside, and the dogs pulled them across the ice.

00:30:47.554 --> 00:30:53.665
The sun came up for half an hour, but it was too cold.

00:30:53.725 --> 00:30:57.676
Minus 46 degrees centigrade.

00:30:57.736 --> 00:31:01.052
They could not travel in that weather.

00:31:01.112 --> 00:31:05.950
They went back to Franheim and waited.

00:31:06.010 --> 00:31:12.074
They waited two weeks until September 8th.

00:31:12.134 --> 00:31:19.859
Then, with the temperature at minus 37 degrees centigrade, they started.

00:31:19.919 --> 00:31:24.252
They ran happily across the snow to the south.

00:31:24.312 --> 00:31:29.692
Eight men, seven sledges, and 86 dogs.

00:31:29.752 --> 00:31:36.731
Only Lindstrom, the cook, stayed behind in Framheim.

00:31:36.790 --> 00:31:40.721
At first, everything went well.

00:31:40.781 --> 00:31:47.493
They went 28 kilometers on Saturday and 28 kilometers on Sunday.

00:31:47.553 --> 00:31:49.644
It was easy.

00:31:49.704 --> 00:31:57.670
Then, on Monday, the temperature went down to minus 56 degrees centigrade.

00:31:57.730 --> 00:32:01.756
There was white fog in front of their faces.

00:32:01.817 --> 00:32:05.148
They couldn't see anything.

00:32:05.208 --> 00:32:09.582
But they traveled 28 kilometers.

00:32:09.642 --> 00:32:15.854
That night, in their tents, they nearly died of cold.

00:32:15.914 --> 00:32:21.088
Next day, they stopped and made snowhouses.

00:32:21.148 --> 00:32:25.220
Inside the snowhouses, it was warm.

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:28.612
But everyone was unhappy.

00:32:28.673 --> 00:32:32.574
I told you, Ruald, you Hanson said.

00:32:32.634 --> 00:32:36.046
Even September is too early.

00:32:36.106 --> 00:32:39.758
We can't travel in this cold.

00:32:39.818 --> 00:32:42.680
Do you want us to die?

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:47.402
Let's go back and wait for better weather."

00:32:47.462 --> 00:32:50.754
Amundsen was very angry.

00:32:50.814 --> 00:32:56.016
He was angry with you Hanson, but he was angry with himself, too.

00:32:56.077 --> 00:33:00.084
He knew you Hanson was right.

00:33:00.144 --> 00:33:04.211
All right, he said slowly.

00:33:04.271 --> 00:33:12.964
We can go on to the depot at 80 degrees south, leave the food there, and then go back.

00:33:13.024 --> 00:33:16.586
We can't do more than that.

00:33:16.646 --> 00:33:21.087
It was 37 kilometers to the depot.

00:33:21.147 --> 00:33:25.529
The wind was in their faces all day.

00:33:25.589 --> 00:33:29.630
Two dogs died on the way.

00:33:29.690 --> 00:33:33.680
At the depot, they did not stop.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:41.071
They put out the food and the flags, turned round, and went north.

00:33:41.131 --> 00:33:44.551
At last, the wind was behind them.

00:33:44.611 --> 00:33:48.932
The dogs ran quickly, and the man sat on the empty sledges.

00:33:48.992 --> 00:33:51.332
They went faster and faster.

00:33:51.392 --> 00:33:53.163
It was like a race.

00:33:53.223 --> 00:34:00.483
Amazon was on Visting's sledge, and soon he, Visting and Honson were three or four kilometers in front.

00:34:00.544 --> 00:34:02.774
Soon, they were alone.

00:34:02.834 --> 00:34:12.106
They traveled 75 kilometers in nine hours, and they reached Framheim at four o'clock that afternoon.

00:34:12.167 --> 00:34:18.118
Beorland arrived two hours later with two more men.

00:34:18.178 --> 00:34:24.678
But the last two, Johansen and Prestrude, went more slowly.

00:34:24.738 --> 00:34:27.255
Their dogs were tired.

00:34:27.316 --> 00:34:29.936
Their feet were wet and cold.

00:34:29.997 --> 00:34:32.107
They had no food.

00:34:32.168 --> 00:34:35.709
And they were alone in the dark.

00:34:35.769 --> 00:34:41.490
The temperature was minus 51 degrees centigrade.

00:34:41.550 --> 00:34:46.302
They reached Framheim at midnight.

00:34:46.363 --> 00:34:51.134
Next morning, Johansson was angry.

00:34:51.194 --> 00:34:57.058
In front of everyone, he said, You were wrong, Ruald.

00:34:57.118 --> 00:35:00.469
September was too early.

00:35:00.529 --> 00:35:04.850
I told you, but you didn't listen.

00:35:04.910 --> 00:35:08.152
And then you left us alone.

00:35:08.212 --> 00:35:11.972
And we nearly died in the cold.

00:35:12.033 --> 00:35:14.964
You're a bad captain.

00:35:15.024 --> 00:35:18.845
I'm a better captain than you are.

00:35:18.905 --> 00:35:22.379
Amundsen was very angry.

00:35:22.439 --> 00:35:31.505
But, at first, he said nothing, because he knew that Johansson was right.

00:35:31.566 --> 00:35:37.548
Then, that evening, he gave a letter to Johansson.

00:35:37.608 --> 00:35:43.080
It said, you aren't coming to the Pole with me.

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:50.902
When I go south, you can take some dogs and go east to King Edward the Seventh Land.

00:35:50.962 --> 00:35:54.783
You can go with Prestrud and Stuberud.

00:35:54.843 --> 00:36:02.145
You can be the first men to go there, but not to the South Pole.

00:36:02.205 --> 00:36:07.396
The Norwegians stayed in Framheim and waited.

00:36:07.456 --> 00:36:21.596
They lay in bed, listened to the wind outside, and thought about Scott and his motor sledges.

00:36:21.656 --> 00:36:27.214
Chapter 7.

00:36:27.274 --> 00:36:35.428
Motor Sledges and Mountains Scott had two motor sledges now.

00:36:35.488 --> 00:36:41.710
They were the first motor sledges in the Antarctic, the first on Earth.

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:48.554
On October 24th, the motor sledges started south from Cape Evans.

00:36:48.614 --> 00:36:56.429
Four men went with them, but Scott stayed at Cape Evans for another week.

00:36:56.489 --> 00:37:00.022
Oats was unhappy.

00:37:00.082 --> 00:37:06.524
He wrote to his mother, we had a very bad winter here.

00:37:06.585 --> 00:37:09.346
I don't like Scott.

00:37:09.406 --> 00:37:16.738
We were here all winter, but he didn't learn to ski or to drive dogs.

00:37:16.799 --> 00:37:23.582
Our equipment is bad, and he doesn't think about other people.

00:37:23.642 --> 00:37:27.723
I'm going to sleep in his tent on the journey.

00:37:27.783 --> 00:37:31.415
but I don't want to.

00:37:31.475 --> 00:37:43.412
On November 1, Scott and Oates and six more men left Cape Evans with eight sledges and eight ponies.

00:37:43.473 --> 00:37:49.657
The ponies walked slowly because their feet went down into the snow.

00:37:49.717 --> 00:37:55.161
It was hard work for them and they got tired very quickly.

00:37:55.222 --> 00:38:00.964
They traveled 13 or 14 kilometers in a day.

00:38:01.024 --> 00:38:08.208
Behind the ponies came meers with one sledge and some dogs.

00:38:08.268 --> 00:38:11.719
Meers knew how to drive dogs.

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:22.744
Every day, meers started two hours after the ponies and arrived two hours before them.

00:38:22.804 --> 00:38:29.600
After five days, they found the motor sledges.

00:38:29.660 --> 00:38:35.365
The Norwegians began again on October 20th.

00:38:35.425 --> 00:38:46.865
There were five men this time, Amundsen, Beorland, Visting, Harsell, and Hanssen.

00:38:46.925 --> 00:38:51.982
They had four sledges and 48 dogs.

00:38:52.042 --> 00:38:56.557
There was a lot of wind and fog.

00:38:56.617 --> 00:39:04.690
On the first day, Visting's sledge suddenly stopped, and the back went down.

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:08.111
Come on, you dogs!

00:39:08.171 --> 00:39:09.772
He said angrily.

00:39:09.832 --> 00:39:10.982
Bull!

00:39:11.042 --> 00:39:12.532
Bull!

00:39:12.592 --> 00:39:15.453
At first nothing happened.

00:39:15.514 --> 00:39:20.456
Then, slowly, the sledge moved again.

00:39:20.516 --> 00:39:24.740
Visting looked down over the side of the sledge.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:29.982
Under the snow there was a fifty meter hole.

00:39:30.042 --> 00:39:32.752
Did you see that?

00:39:32.812 --> 00:39:34.473
Amundsen said.

00:39:34.533 --> 00:39:36.984
The ice wants to eat us.

00:39:37.044 --> 00:39:42.246
Men, dogs, sledges, everything.

00:39:42.306 --> 00:39:48.658
On the fourth day they reached the depot at eighty degrees south.

00:39:48.718 --> 00:39:51.259
There was a bad snowstorm.

00:39:51.320 --> 00:39:54.682
but they found the flags easily.

00:39:54.742 --> 00:40:02.558
Next day, the men stayed in their tents and the dogs played in their holes under the snow.

00:40:02.618 --> 00:40:05.070
They were all happy.

00:40:05.130 --> 00:40:10.044
They had a lot of food, they had good equipment and they were warm.

00:40:10.104 --> 00:40:13.197
They could travel fast.

00:40:13.257 --> 00:40:20.749
Next morning, the snow storm stopped and the journey began again.

00:40:20.809 --> 00:40:24.987
Today, everything is wonderful.

00:40:25.047 --> 00:40:27.638
The Orland wrote in his diary.

00:40:27.698 --> 00:40:35.010
But where is Scott in front of us or behind?

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:39.431
There was no one with the motor sledges.

00:40:39.492 --> 00:40:41.462
They were broken.

00:40:41.522 --> 00:40:44.893
Scott looked at them angrily.

00:40:44.953 --> 00:40:48.494
It doesn't matter, he said.

00:40:48.554 --> 00:40:51.748
Teddy Evans and his men are in front of us.

00:40:51.808 --> 00:40:53.543
They're good men.

00:40:53.603 --> 00:40:56.596
They're pulling their sledges themselves.

00:40:56.656 --> 00:41:01.109
We can get to the pole on foot.

00:41:01.169 --> 00:41:04.382
Oats looked at Mears.

00:41:04.442 --> 00:41:09.886
Oats and the ponies were tired, but Mears and his dogs were not.

00:41:09.946 --> 00:41:12.979
The snow was home for them.

00:41:13.039 --> 00:41:20.241
That night, Oats wrote, three motor sledges at 1,000 pounds each.

00:41:20.301 --> 00:41:25.732
19 ponies at five pounds each.

00:41:25.772 --> 00:41:29.516
32 dogs at one pound fifty each.

00:41:29.576 --> 00:41:34.871
Well, it's not my money, it's Scott's.

00:41:34.931 --> 00:41:41.608
On November 21st, one of the ponies died.

00:41:41.668 --> 00:41:47.548
On November 11th, the Norwegians saw the mountains.

00:41:47.608 --> 00:41:53.304
The mountains were very high, some of the highest on earth.

00:41:53.364 --> 00:41:55.774
The Orland smiled.

00:41:55.835 --> 00:41:59.685
There is good skiing up there, Ruhald, he said.

00:41:59.745 --> 00:42:02.596
But can dogs get up there too?

00:42:02.656 --> 00:42:06.096
Of course they can, Amundsen said.

00:42:06.156 --> 00:42:08.007
Come on.

00:42:08.067 --> 00:42:14.045
They left Hanssen with the dogs and ski the little way up the mountains.

00:42:14.106 --> 00:42:19.774
It was difficult, but the mountains were big and beautiful.

00:42:19.834 --> 00:42:26.086
Behind the mountains, Amazon thought there was a high plateau of ice.

00:42:26.147 --> 00:42:29.697
That's it, Amazon said.

00:42:29.758 --> 00:42:32.738
That's the road to the pole.

00:42:32.799 --> 00:42:37.180
Tomorrow, we can bring the dogs and sledges up here.

00:42:37.240 --> 00:42:41.211
But now, let's have a ski race.

00:42:41.271 --> 00:42:44.531
Who can get back to camp first?

00:42:44.591 --> 00:42:49.631
They laughed and skied happily down the white snow.

00:42:49.691 --> 00:42:57.883
This is like home, be all on thought, but it's bigger than Norway and better.

00:42:57.943 --> 00:43:07.753
In the next four days, the dogs pulled the sledges 81 kilometres and went up 3000 metres.

00:43:07.813 --> 00:43:15.144
At last, Amundsen and Beorland stood on the plateau behind the mountains.

00:43:15.204 --> 00:43:18.916
They were tired, happy men.

00:43:18.976 --> 00:43:22.788
Beorland looked back at the mountains.

00:43:22.848 --> 00:43:25.880
Can a motor sledge get up here?

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:27.560
He asked.

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:30.282
Amundsen smiled.

00:43:30.342 --> 00:43:32.784
No, he said.

00:43:32.844 --> 00:43:34.884
I don't think so.

00:43:34.944 --> 00:43:37.874
And Scott doesn't like dogs.

00:43:37.934 --> 00:43:43.825
So his men are going to pull their sledges up these mountains themselves.

00:43:43.885 --> 00:43:46.956
Would you like to do that, Ulaf?"

00:43:47.016 --> 00:43:49.748
Biorland didn't answer.

00:43:49.808 --> 00:43:58.643
He smiled and skied happily away across the snow.

00:43:58.704 --> 00:44:13.782
Chapter 8 Across the Plateau On November 21st, the Norwegians killed 30 dogs.

00:44:13.842 --> 00:44:19.375
They were happy, Amundsen said, and now they're going to die quickly.

00:44:19.436 --> 00:44:25.289
We need three sledges and 18 dogs to go to the pole.

00:44:25.349 --> 00:44:29.582
When the dogs were dead, the other dogs ate them.

00:44:29.642 --> 00:44:32.442
The men ate them too.

00:44:32.502 --> 00:44:36.820
They were good friends, Beorland wrote in his diary.

00:44:36.880 --> 00:44:40.316
and now they are good food.

00:44:40.377 --> 00:44:44.698
Two days later, the dogs were fat.

00:44:44.758 --> 00:44:50.400
Then, in the snowstorm, they began their journey again.

00:44:50.460 --> 00:45:00.224
After the snowstorm, there was fog, and in the fog, they got lost on an ice river with hundreds of big holes in it.

00:45:00.284 --> 00:45:05.538
They could see nothing, and it was very dangerous.

00:45:05.598 --> 00:45:10.752
In four days, they moved nine kilometers.

00:45:10.812 --> 00:45:15.486
But the ice is beautiful, be all hand-wrote.

00:45:15.546 --> 00:45:18.618
Blue and green and white.

00:45:18.678 --> 00:45:25.762
This is a wonderful place, but I don't want to stay a long time.

00:45:25.822 --> 00:45:31.656
After the ice, there were strong winds and bad snowstorms.

00:45:31.716 --> 00:45:34.852
They could see nothing in front of them.

00:45:34.912 --> 00:45:40.828
but every day they traveled 25 or 30 kilometers.

00:45:40.888 --> 00:45:46.289
Then, on December 9th, the sun came out.

00:45:46.349 --> 00:45:56.131
They were at 88 degrees, 23 minutes south, 175 kilometers from the pole.

00:45:56.191 --> 00:46:02.512
Five more long days, Bjorland wrote, that's all now.

00:46:02.572 --> 00:46:06.348
But where is Scott?

00:46:06.408 --> 00:46:13.344
For four days, Scott's men stayed in their tents near the mountains.

00:46:13.404 --> 00:46:18.186
There is a bad snowstorm outside, Oates wrote.

00:46:18.246 --> 00:46:24.568
It's too cold for the ponies, and our clothes and skis are bad too.

00:46:24.629 --> 00:46:29.390
On December 9th, Oates killed the ponies.

00:46:29.450 --> 00:46:34.790
They were tired and ill, and they could not walk up to the plateau.

00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:39.210
Their mirrors and his dogs went back to Cape Evans.

00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:43.382
We can pull the sledges ourselves, Scott said.

00:46:43.442 --> 00:46:44.912
We can do it.

00:46:44.972 --> 00:46:47.802
We're all strong men.

00:46:47.862 --> 00:46:51.834
There were two sledges and eight men.

00:46:51.894 --> 00:46:55.496
They went 24 kilometers a day.

00:46:55.556 --> 00:47:04.414
On December 31, Scott said to Teddy Evans and the men on the second sledge, you can't ski well.

00:47:04.474 --> 00:47:06.570
Leave your skis here."

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:12.322
So they pulled their sledge 24 kilometers without skis.

00:47:12.382 --> 00:47:17.174
Next day, Scott went to Teddy Evans' tent.

00:47:17.234 --> 00:47:20.306
You're ill, Teddy, he said.

00:47:20.366 --> 00:47:22.786
You can't come to the pole.

00:47:22.846 --> 00:47:26.947
Take two men and go back, tomorrow.

00:47:27.008 --> 00:47:30.419
Teddy Evans was very unhappy.

00:47:30.479 --> 00:47:33.837
Two men, Captain, he said.

00:47:33.897 --> 00:47:36.055
Why not three?

00:47:36.115 --> 00:47:40.568
Because Bowers is going to come with me, Scott said.

00:47:40.628 --> 00:47:41.818
Be strong.

00:47:41.879 --> 00:47:43.390
We need him.

00:47:43.450 --> 00:47:50.474
But you have food on your sledge for four men, not five, Evan said.

00:47:50.534 --> 00:47:53.676
And Bowers has no skis.

00:47:53.736 --> 00:47:57.558
I'm the captain, Teddy, Scott said.

00:47:57.618 --> 00:47:59.980
You do what I say.

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:04.158
Take two men and leave Bowers with me.

00:48:04.218 --> 00:48:07.165
Oats wrote to his mother.

00:48:07.225 --> 00:48:10.116
I am going to the pole with Scott.

00:48:10.176 --> 00:48:13.378
I am pleased and I feel strong.

00:48:13.438 --> 00:48:19.830
But in his diary he wrote, my feet are very bad.

00:48:19.890 --> 00:48:25.742
They are always wet now, and they don't look good.

00:48:25.803 --> 00:48:32.310
On January 4th, Scott's man left Teddy Evans and went on.

00:48:32.370 --> 00:48:40.440
Scott, Oates, Wilson and Edgar Evans had skis, but Bowers did not.

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:45.973
They were 270 kilometers from the pole.

00:48:46.034 --> 00:48:52.967
December 14, 1911 was a warm, sunny day.

00:48:53.028 --> 00:48:57.670
Five Norwegians skied over the beautiful white snow.

00:48:57.730 --> 00:49:00.052
It was very quiet.

00:49:00.112 --> 00:49:02.012
No one spoke.

00:49:02.072 --> 00:49:05.342
They were excited and happy.

00:49:05.402 --> 00:49:09.856
Six more kilometers, be all unthought.

00:49:09.916 --> 00:49:12.396
Is there a British flag?

00:49:12.456 --> 00:49:15.368
I can't see a flag, but...

00:49:15.428 --> 00:49:18.090
Look, Harsel said.

00:49:18.150 --> 00:49:20.392
What's that over there?

00:49:20.452 --> 00:49:25.444
Be all armed, left his sledge, and see quickly away over the snow.

00:49:25.504 --> 00:49:26.805
What is it?

00:49:26.865 --> 00:49:27.755
He thought.

00:49:27.816 --> 00:49:28.936
Is it?

00:49:28.996 --> 00:49:30.575
No.

00:49:30.635 --> 00:49:33.454
It's nothing, he called.

00:49:33.514 --> 00:49:35.525
There's nothing there.

00:49:35.586 --> 00:49:37.717
Nothing.

00:49:37.778 --> 00:49:41.370
Three kilometers, two.

00:49:41.430 --> 00:49:43.041
Ruwald!

00:49:43.101 --> 00:49:45.402
Hanssen called to Amundsen.

00:49:45.462 --> 00:49:47.644
Go in front of me, please.

00:49:47.704 --> 00:49:49.956
It helps my dogs.

00:49:50.016 --> 00:49:51.957
That's not true.

00:49:52.017 --> 00:49:53.548
Be all on foot.

00:49:53.608 --> 00:49:56.130
His dogs are running well today.

00:49:56.190 --> 00:49:59.637
But Hanssen wants Amundsen to be first.

00:49:59.697 --> 00:50:03.634
the first man at the South Pole.

00:50:03.694 --> 00:50:09.308
They skied on and on, over the beautiful snow.

00:50:09.368 --> 00:50:11.188
Stop!

00:50:11.248 --> 00:50:13.180
Amundsen said.

00:50:13.240 --> 00:50:16.562
He waited quietly for his men.

00:50:16.622 --> 00:50:21.584
This is it, he said.

00:50:21.644 --> 00:50:24.096
We all aren't looked at him.

00:50:24.156 --> 00:50:27.388
But there's nothing here, he said.

00:50:27.448 --> 00:50:29.774
Amundsen smiled.

00:50:29.834 --> 00:50:33.340
Oh, yes, there is, he said.

00:50:33.400 --> 00:50:37.842
There's something very important here, Ulaf.

00:50:37.902 --> 00:50:41.354
Very, very important.

00:50:41.414 --> 00:50:43.906
What's that, Ruav?

00:50:43.966 --> 00:50:47.707
Us, we are here now.

00:50:47.767 --> 00:50:50.838
Isn't that important, Ulaf?

00:50:50.899 --> 00:50:56.240
The four men stood on the snow and looked at him.

00:50:56.301 --> 00:51:07.033
Then, slowly, They all began to laugh.

00:51:07.093 --> 00:51:21.460
Chapter 9 The End of the Race The Norwegians stayed two days at the pole.

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:26.302
They left a tent there with a Norwegian flag on it.

00:51:26.362 --> 00:51:29.790
Inside the tent, they left some food.

00:51:29.850 --> 00:51:34.429
A letter for the King of Norway, and a letter for Scott.

00:51:34.490 --> 00:51:41.354
They left some more black flags near the pole, and won 28 kilometers north.

00:51:41.415 --> 00:51:46.567
Then they skied away, back to the north.

00:51:46.628 --> 00:51:51.040
It's a beautiful day, Beorland Road.

00:51:51.100 --> 00:51:54.592
The sun is warm, the snow is good.

00:51:54.652 --> 00:51:57.214
But the dogs run too quickly.

00:51:57.274 --> 00:51:59.780
I can't get in front of them.

00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:02.926
They found their depots easily.

00:52:02.986 --> 00:52:06.548
There were ten between the pole and Framheim.

00:52:06.608 --> 00:52:09.829
Each depot had a lot of food.

00:52:09.889 --> 00:52:13.910
They laughed and skied quickly down the mountains.

00:52:13.970 --> 00:52:18.652
Often they skied 50 kilometers a day.

00:52:18.712 --> 00:52:27.096
On Friday, January 26, 1912, they came back to Framheim.

00:52:27.156 --> 00:52:30.450
It was four o'clock in the morning.

00:52:30.510 --> 00:52:36.235
Inside the wooden house, Lindstrom, the cook, was asleep.

00:52:36.295 --> 00:52:39.896
Amundsen walked quietly to his bed.

00:52:39.956 --> 00:52:43.828
Good morning, Lindstrom, he said.

00:52:43.888 --> 00:52:47.319
Is our coffee ready?

00:52:47.379 --> 00:52:52.522
The black flags waited at the pole.

00:52:52.582 --> 00:52:55.002
What's that, Captain?

00:52:55.062 --> 00:52:58.254
Bauer said, over there.

00:52:58.314 --> 00:52:59.715
Where?

00:52:59.775 --> 00:53:00.916
Scott asked.

00:53:00.976 --> 00:53:02.326
What?

00:53:02.386 --> 00:53:05.197
Oh my God!

00:53:05.258 --> 00:53:13.632
They all saw the small black flag in the snow, two kilometers in front of them.

00:53:13.692 --> 00:53:18.754
Slowly, they pulled their sledge to it.

00:53:18.813 --> 00:53:28.489
Next day, January 17th, 1912, they found the tent and the Norwegian flag.

00:53:28.549 --> 00:53:35.605
Near it, Scott took the British flag from under his clothes and put it up.

00:53:35.666 --> 00:53:43.500
In his diary, Scott wrote, this is a very bad day.

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:49.334
We are all tired and have cold feet and hands.

00:53:49.395 --> 00:53:53.128
It is minus 30 degrees centigrade.

00:53:53.188 --> 00:53:56.700
And there is a snowstorm.

00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:59.258
Great God.

00:53:59.319 --> 00:54:04.307
This is an awful place.

00:54:04.367 --> 00:54:17.520
They turned north, five tired, unhappy men, in the coldest, emptiest place on earth.

00:54:17.580 --> 00:54:34.141
On March 13, 1912, Scott's wife, Kathleen, looked at her morning newspaper, Norway's flag at South Pole, it said.

00:54:34.202 --> 00:54:40.436
She looked at it for a long time, and then began to cry.

00:54:40.496 --> 00:54:42.547
What's the matter?

00:54:42.607 --> 00:54:44.718
Her friend asked.

00:54:44.779 --> 00:54:50.052
My poor, poor husband, Mrs. Scott said.

00:54:50.112 --> 00:54:52.544
What's happened to him?

00:54:52.604 --> 00:54:55.966
Where is he now?

00:54:56.026 --> 00:54:59.930
Scots men were always hungry.

00:54:59.990 --> 00:55:04.042
There were not many depots, and they were difficult to find.

00:55:04.102 --> 00:55:09.164
We need to find the next depot today, oats wrote.

00:55:09.224 --> 00:55:15.097
But how can we find one black flag in all this snow?

00:55:15.157 --> 00:55:17.608
It's very difficult.

00:55:17.668 --> 00:55:23.132
And there is food for four men, not five.

00:55:23.192 --> 00:55:27.236
They were all tired and ill too.

00:55:27.297 --> 00:55:32.844
Oates' feet were black now, and he could not feel them.

00:55:32.904 --> 00:55:38.527
On February 16th, Edgar Evans died.

00:55:38.588 --> 00:55:43.281
On the 17th, they were past the mountains.

00:55:43.341 --> 00:55:47.714
At the depot there, they ate one of the dead ponies.

00:55:47.774 --> 00:55:49.305
Then they went on.

00:55:49.325 --> 00:55:54.772
10, 11, 12 kilometers a day.

00:55:54.832 --> 00:56:01.737
They were ill because their clothes were not warm, and they didn't have much food.

00:56:01.797 --> 00:56:07.720
The temperature was sometimes minus 40 degrees centigrade.

00:56:07.780 --> 00:56:12.711
On March 7th, Scott looked at Oates' feet.

00:56:12.771 --> 00:56:15.572
They were big and black.

00:56:15.632 --> 00:56:20.154
I can't pull the sledge now, Oates said.

00:56:20.214 --> 00:56:22.975
It's very difficult to walk.

00:56:23.035 --> 00:56:26.944
Am I going to lose these feet, Captain?

00:56:27.004 --> 00:56:33.796
Scott looked at Oates' feet and said nothing.

00:56:33.856 --> 00:56:40.870
On March 9th, they found another depot, but there was not much food.

00:56:40.930 --> 00:56:44.371
Slowly, they walked on.

00:56:44.432 --> 00:56:48.714
Oates' feet were worse every day.

00:56:48.774 --> 00:56:53.386
March 17th was Oates' birthday.

00:56:53.446 --> 00:56:55.690
He was 32.

00:56:55.750 --> 00:57:01.084
He lay in the tent and listened to the wind outside.

00:57:01.144 --> 00:57:08.818
He was very cold, very hungry, and very, very tired.

00:57:08.878 --> 00:57:14.660
He wrote a letter to his mother and gave it to Wilson.

00:57:14.720 --> 00:57:19.752
Then he got up and opened the door of the tent.

00:57:19.812 --> 00:57:23.104
He stopped in the door for a minute.

00:57:23.164 --> 00:57:27.338
Scott, Wilson and Bowers looked at him.

00:57:27.398 --> 00:57:29.970
They didn't speak.

00:57:30.031 --> 00:57:34.732
I'm going outside for a minute, out said.

00:57:34.792 --> 00:57:37.854
And maybe some time.

00:57:37.914 --> 00:57:42.776
They didn't see him again.

00:57:42.836 --> 00:57:48.318
At Cape Evans, the Englishman waited.

00:57:48.378 --> 00:57:53.271
On December 11th, Mears and the Dogs came back.

00:57:53.331 --> 00:57:59.994
On January 3, Teddy Evans and his two men arrived at Cape Evans.

00:58:00.054 --> 00:58:03.934
The Terranova came and went.

00:58:03.994 --> 00:58:06.225
Winter began.

00:58:06.285 --> 00:58:09.766
Scott did not come.

00:58:09.826 --> 00:58:14.516
The Englishmen waited all winter at Cape Evans.

00:58:14.576 --> 00:58:20.878
Then, on October 26, 1912, they started for the South.

00:58:20.938 --> 00:58:26.490
Two weeks later, they found a tent.

00:58:26.550 --> 00:58:29.720
There were three bodies in the tent.

00:58:29.781 --> 00:58:34.202
Scott, Wilson, and Bowers.

00:58:34.262 --> 00:58:37.903
They put the bodies under the snow.

00:58:37.963 --> 00:58:44.654
Then they took the men's letters and diaries and went north to Cape Evans again.

00:58:44.714 --> 00:58:51.656
In Scott's diary, they read, oats died like a good Englishman.

00:58:51.716 --> 00:58:53.564
We all did.

00:58:53.624 --> 00:58:59.452
Please remember us and look after our families.

00:58:59.512 --> 00:59:03.192
We did our best.

00:59:03.252 --> 00:59:17.367
No one found out his body, but he is there somewhere under the snow and the wind in the coldest, emptiest place on earth.

